For an empty array, `reduce(..., 0)` returns `0` and `xs.length` is `0`, so the function computes `0 / 0`, which is **`NaN`**.

Add an explicit empty-array guard. Since an empty set has no defined average, throwing is a clear default:

```js
function average(xs) {
  if (xs.length === 0) {
    throw new RangeError("Cannot average an empty array");
  }
  return xs.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / xs.length;
}
```

If callers expect a missing-value result instead, return `null` for empty input and document that contract. Avoid returning `0` unless your application explicitly defines that behavior—it can hide missing data.
